      "message": "Thank you for this improvement. I have a few questions regarding reliability, failure handling, synchronization, and the overall scope of this implementation.\n\n1) Crash safety, power failures, and recovery\n\nWhat happens if one or more Jami clients hang, become completely unresponsive, get stuck in a deadlock, freeze during an operation, or stop processing incoming or outgoing messages while the underlying processes are still running?\n\nWhat happens if one or more clients crash, lose their connection, or the entire system suddenly loses power at any point during the creation, queuing, synchronization, transmission, or processing of a read receipt?\n\nIn particular, are queued read receipts persisted reliably and guaranteed to survive client crashes, restarts, extended offline periods, and unexpected power failures?\n\nCould an interruption result in lost receipts, duplicate receipts, outdated read statuses, or receipts remaining queued indefinitely?\n\nAre these operations designed to be sufficiently atomic and idempotent to handle interruptions and retries safely?\n\n2) Consistency and recovery across the sender\u0027s and recipient\u0027s devices\n\nHow are these scenarios handled on both sides of the communication?\n\nFor example, what happens if the recipient\u0027s client successfully reads a message and queues the corresponding read receipt, but crashes before the receipt is transmitted?\n\nIs the receipt reliably sent once the recipient reconnects, even if the conversation is no longer open?\n\nLikewise, what happens if the sender\u0027s client is offline, hangs, or crashes while a read receipt is being received, processed, or synchronized across the sender\u0027s own devices?\n\nIs the read status reliably recovered and synchronized after a restart or reconnection, without losing newer status information or allowing an older receipt to overwrite a newer one?\n\n3) Multiple devices and concurrent read events\n\nHow are simultaneous or nearly simultaneous read events handled when multiple devices belonging to the same account read the same message or different messages?\n\nFor example, what happens if one device queues a read receipt while another device independently reads the same message or generates a newer receipt?\n\nIs the refresh-token mechanism guaranteed to handle concurrent updates atomically, so that an older pending receipt cannot accidentally replace a newer one or cause newer read-status information to be lost?\n\nWhat happens if a previous receipt has already been transmitted but its successful processing or synchronization has not yet been communicated to the originating client?\n\nIs it also guaranteed that read-status information can never regress to an older state due to delayed, duplicated, or out-of-order receipts arriving from different devices? In other words, can the system reliably distinguish newer from older read-status updates and ensure that an outdated receipt never overwrites more recent information?\n\n4) Scope: 1:1 conversations, group conversations, and other conversation types\n\nDoes this implementation apply consistently to all conversation types, including 1:1 Swarms, group Swarms, and any other supported types of conversations?\n\nIn group conversations, are read receipts queued and tracked independently for each member and conversation?\n\nIs it guaranteed that a receipt intended for one member cannot accidentally be delivered to another member, and that newer receipts correctly replace older pending receipts without affecting other members\u0027 receipt states?\n\nAre there any conversation types or communication paths that are intentionally excluded from this implementation?\n\nAccount deletion, blocking, and device removal\n\nWhat happens if one or both accounts involved in the communication are deleted, blocked, or otherwise become unavailable while a read receipt is being created, queued, synchronized, or transmitted?\n\nFor example:\n\nWhat happens if the sender or recipient deletes their Jami account while read receipts are still pending?\nWhat happens if either account blocks the other while a receipt is queued or being transmitted?\nHow is this handled if an account blocks another account on only one of its devices?\nWhat happens if one or more devices belonging to either account are removed, unlinked, or otherwise lose their association with the account while receipts are pending?\n\nAre pending receipts invalidated or discarded when they are no longer relevant or when communication is no longer permitted?\n\nIn particular, could previously queued read receipts unexpectedly be delivered after an account is unblocked, a device reconnects, or an account is restored?\n\n5) Message and conversation deletion\n\nWhat happens if a message or the entire conversation is deleted after a read receipt has been queued but before it has been transmitted?\n\nAre such pending receipts still sent, or are they reliably discarded when they are no longer relevant?\n\nIs this behavior consistent across all devices belonging to the affected accounts?\n\n6) Long-term offline periods and queue management\n\nWhat happens if a recipient or sender remains offline for an extended period, potentially weeks or months?\n\nAre pending receipts retained indefinitely, or is there a mechanism for expiring and cleaning up outdated or no longer relevant entries?\n\nHow is it ensured that the queue does not grow indefinitely or consume unnecessary storage when devices remain unreachable for long periods?\n\nHow are connection requests and retry attempts handled when the recipient remains unreachable for an extended period? Is there a controlled retry mechanism with appropriate backoff and resource management to prevent excessive connection attempts, unnecessary resource consumption, or indefinitely repeated retries?\n\n7) Typing indicators and other message-related status notifications\n\nDoes this change also cover typing indicators, or are they handled through a separate mechanism?\n\nIf typing indicators are not covered, are they also subject to similar reliability issues when the recipient is offline or the message channel is not currently open?\n\nShould a separate mechanism be implemented for typing indicators and other message-related status notifications where appropriate?\n\nI understand that typing indicators are generally transient and therefore may require different handling from persistent read receipts. Nevertheless, it would be useful to clarify whether similar delivery and recovery issues exist there.\n\n8) Interaction with existing implementations and backward compatibility\n\nHave these failure scenarios and consistency requirements also been checked against existing Jami implementations and mechanisms outside the scope of this patch that interact with message delivery, conversation synchronization, read receipts, or message status management?\n\nCould this change introduce incompatibilities or expose existing weaknesses in other components, particularly regarding crash recovery, offline synchronization, concurrent processing, or synchronization between multiple devices belonging to the same account?\n\nWhere necessary, have corresponding safeguards or adjustments to existing implementations also been considered or implemented to ensure that the new mechanism works reliably across the entire system, rather than only within the scope of this patch?\n\nIn other words, are the new mechanisms fully compatible with the existing message delivery, synchronization, and recovery architecture, and have potential issues in related components outside this patch\u0027s scope also been identified and addressed where necessary?\n\nI believe it is important to ensure that read receipts and related message-status information remain reliable and consistent across all relevant devices and conversation types, even when clients are offline, temporarily unreachable, unresponsive, blocked, removed, or unexpectedly interrupted.\n\n9) Mixed client versions and backward compatibility\n\nHow does this implementation behave when only some of the participating Jami clients have this change implemented, while others are still running older versions?\n\nFor example, what happens if the sender uses a client with the new queued read-receipt mechanism, while the recipient or one or more of their other devices still use an older implementation?\n\nIs the new mechanism fully backward-compatible with older clients, including scenarios involving multiple devices belonging to the same account and group conversations where different members may be running different versions?\n\nCould mixed client versions result in lost, duplicated, delayed, or inconsistent read receipts or message-status information?\n\nHow are pending receipts handled when a client is upgraded from an older version to one containing this change, or when a previously upgraded client reconnects to devices that have not yet been upgraded?\n\nAre there any protocol, message-format, or synchronization compatibility requirements, and is this mixed-version behavior explicitly covered by automated tests?\n\n10) Multi-device synchronization, self-conversations, and previously reported issues\n\nThere have been previously reported issues with synchronization and message delivery when using the same Jami account across multiple devices, particularly when some devices are online while others are offline, and when switching between devices at different times.\n\nFor example, GitLab issue #1107 (https://git.jami.net/savoirfairelinux/jami-daemon/-/work_items/1107) describes scenarios where messages are not immediately delivered to all devices belonging to a contact, message histories become incorrectly ordered or mixed up, and message delivery and read statuses are not synchronized reliably when devices reconnect or switch between online and offline states.\n\nCould you please verify whether the issues described in #1107 have already been fully resolved and whether this patch is compatible with the existing multi-device synchronization mechanisms?\n\nIn particular:\n\nWhat happens when two or more devices belonging to the same Jami account communicate with each other using the same account URI (e.g. Account A, Device 1 ↔ Account A, Device 2 ↔ Account A, Device 3), with some devices being offline temporarily or for extended periods?\nAre read receipts reliably generated, queued, transmitted, and synchronized across all devices in these scenarios?\nDoes the special connected-only path for the account\u0027s own URI introduce any limitations or potential synchronization problems when the other devices belonging to the same account are offline or unreachable?\nAre message delivery statuses, read statuses, and message ordering preserved correctly when devices reconnect after different periods of being offline?\nAre similar synchronization issues handled correctly when the other conversation participant also uses multiple devices, either simultaneously or at different times?\nAre typing indicators also handled correctly in these multi-device and self-conversation scenarios, or are there existing issues that need to be addressed separately?\n\nHave the existing implementations and related synchronization mechanisms also been reviewed and corrected where necessary, rather than only addressing the issue within the scope of this patch?\n\nAre these scenarios explicitly covered by automated tests, including different combinations of online and offline devices, interrupted connections, multiple devices belonging to the same account, and communication between devices using the same account URI?",
